# Root-level ignores. `android/` and `server/` each carry their own, more specific rules —
# this file covers the repo root and adds a defence-in-depth net that applies at every depth.

# Build caches. The Gradle project moved into android/, so anything left here is stale.
/build
/.gradle
/.kotlin

# AI session scratch state (.remember/ also self-ignores via its own .gitignore)
.loki/
.remember/

# Editors / OS
.idea/
*.iml
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ─── Defence in depth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None of these should ever be committed. They are listed without a leading slash
# so they match at ANY depth, including inside android/ and server/. This is
# deliberately redundant with the per-directory .gitignore files: a public repo is
# unforgiving, and the cost of a duplicated rule is nothing next to the cost of a
# leaked key.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
service-account.json
*-service-account.json
*.key.json
google-services.json
keystore.properties
local.properties
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pem
*.key

# SQLite holds real conversation history, device tokens and pairing secrets.
# The -wal and -shm sidecars do NOT match *.sqlite and routinely carry megabytes
# of rows not yet checkpointed into the main file.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
*.db

# Anchored to the server, NOT matched at every depth: the Android app has a source
# package at android/app/src/main/java/com/otto/app/data/, and a bare `data/` rule
# would silently swallow every new file added to it.
/server/data/
/server/backups/
